so a t is a t is a t and thus the  japanese t must be identical to the  english t right at least this is what  we're typically taught in japanese  textbooks and japanese classrooms but  this actually isn't true um there are  two major differences between the  japanese tea and the english tea one of  which is aspiration sort of the amount  of air that we exhale when saying the t  sounds as well as the kmp sounds for  that matter aspiration however is a  little bit complicated and i've talked  about it in detail at length so i'll  just link that video down below but the  other thing is actually tongue placement  so  in the english tea if you say the word  ted the name ted for example try saying  that a few times  ted  ted ted noticing where you're where the  tip of your tongue is touching  ted  ted  so it's touching what's called the  alveolar ridge kind of that bump just  behind our upper front teeth  ted ted again if you try and say it  again with that knowledge you'll almost  certainly notice that in the japanese  tea the tip of the tongue comes in  contact with the back of the upper front  teeth  and then the blade of the tongue so kind  of the area just behind the tip of the  tongue if this is the tip of the tongue  here  then the blade of the tongue is right  here  [Music]  again so kind of just right behind the  tip of the tongue on the top but the tip  of the tongue is touching the back of  the upper front teeth and then the blade  of the tongue is touching the alveolar  ridge so in that sense the japanese tea  has just a slightly more forward tongue  position than the english tea and if i  say the word for generally in japanese  which is spelled like this  you'll almost notice immediately  if you're looking for it and this is  funny that you can actually see the  difference  that you can see more of my tongue as i  say the pet part of this word so i'll  say it a few times now  now i'm kind of exaggerating it a little  bit for the effect but again if you look  for  this more forward tongue position  in native speech you'll start to notice  it i had a conversation with a japanese  friend during study abroad and i was  trying to say that word actually which  is why i'm using it as an example and he  was saying your t sound sounds slightly  off and i said my t sounds and he said  yeah like you're saying ippon  teki teki ipanteki ipanteki but it's not  iponteki it's  and i remember not being able to hear  the difference but when he when he said  it i was like oh his tongue is more  forward in that second one i'm seeing  more of his tongue kind of in between  the sides of his uh well on the sides of  his upper front teeth and again this is  because the tongue placement is  different so just to summarize things in  the japanese tea the tip of the tongue  is touching the back of the upper front  teeth and the blade of the tongue is  touching the alveolar ridge this is  different from the english tea in which  only the tip of the tongue is touching  the alveolar ridge at least in american  english i hope that this was useful i  teach japanese pronunciation on patreon  linked below if anyone's interested in  that good luck with your japanese  studies and talk to all you guys again  soon
 

